      <h2>Why Conventional Computing Is Bloated—and What Hyperfactoring Actually Is</h2>
      <p>
        Ask yourself why a modern AI training cluster, a molecular docking pipeline, a financial matching engine, a lossless compressor, and a decentralized network look like five completely different industries—each requiring millions of lines of C++, CUDA, Python, Linux kernel drivers, Docker containers, and ad-hoc test suites.
      </p>
      <p>
        They are not five different problems. Every single one of those bottlenecks arises from forgetting the exact algebraic structure of the computation and paying for that amnesia in memory, latency, and bugs. <strong>Hyperfactoring</strong> is the discipline of finding the minimal algebraic core—usually 20 to 50 lines of typed cubical code—where the proof of correctness <em>is</em> the optimization, and compiling that exact specification straight to machine code with zero glue layers.
      </p>

