      <div class="ch-num">Chapter 02 · Information &amp; Compression</div>
      <h2>Information as Causal Structure: Exact Streaming Context-Fiber Compression (<code>hxz</code>)</h2>
      <p>
        Why can a file be compressed at all? Shannon proved that if the next byte $x_t$ given its past context $c_t = (x_{t-1}, x_{t-2}, \dots)$ has probability $p(x_t \mid c_t)$, then you need only $-\log_2 p(x_t \mid c_t)$ bits to transmit it—not 8 bits. Standard compressors like <code>gzip</code> hunt for verbatim copy-paste substrings (LZ77) and pay whole-bit Huffman rounding tax.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hyperactivity's <code>hxz</code> (<code>lib/hxz.hypercubical</code> over <code>lib/compress-stream.hypercubical</code>) works directly from information theory in two clean pieces:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Causal Context Fibers (Orders 4 → 3 → 2 → 1 → 0 → Ground):</strong> Before reading byte $x_t$, both encoder and decoder hold the exact same causal history. We query the deepest context matching the last 4 bytes. If $x_t$ was seen in that fiber, we code it directly with its conditional frequency $f/M$; otherwise we emit an <em>escape</em> symbol, add every symbol already ruled out at depth $k$ into an exclusion set (so lower depths never waste probability mass on impossible bytes!), and fall back to depth $k-1$.</li>
        <li><strong>Streaming Renormalized rANS (Asymmetric Numeral Systems):</strong> Instead of building an $O(N)$-bit bignum, rANS keeps a single 64-bit integer state $s$ strictly inside $[L, 256 L)$, encoding symbol $x$ with cumulative frequency $C$ and count $f$ out of total $M$ by:
      </ol>

      <div class="eq-box">
        $$s_{\text{next}} = \left\lfloor \frac{s}{f} \right\rfloor M + C + (s \bmod f), \qquad \text{inverse:}\quad r = s_{\text{next}} \bmod M,\;\; s = \left\lfloor \frac{s_{\text{next}}}{M} \right\rfloor f + (r - C)$$
      </div>

      <div class="thm-box">
        <div class="thm-head"><span>The Exact Renormalization Divisibility Theorem</span><span>Why $M \mid L$ Is Mandatory</span></div>
        <p style="margin-bottom:10px">
          Before encoding a symbol of frequency $f$, the encoder flushes base-256 bytes while $s \ge \mathrm{hi}(f) = \lfloor 256 L / M \rfloor \cdot f$. After decoding, the decoder refills bytes while $s < L$. When does $s_{\text{next}}$ stay guaranteed inside $[L, 256L)$ for <em>every</em> possible byte stream?
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0">
          Write $L = qM + r$ with $1 \le r < M$. Then $256 \lceil L/M \rceil = 256q + 256 > 256q + \lfloor 256r/M \rfloor = \lfloor 256L/M \rfloor$. Whenever $M \nmid L$, there exists an exact boundary state where flushing drops $s_{\text{next}}$ just below $L$! Setting $L = 32768 = 2^{15}$ and padding the escape count in 5 lines (<code>hxz-pow2</code>, <code>hxz-ft</code>) so $M = 2^k$ divides $L$ makes $256 L / M$ <strong>exact with zero remainder</strong>—guaranteeing bit-exact lossless round-trip for every file of every size in the universe.
        </p>
      </div>

